<h2>Fast transit, and a re-export dimension worth understanding</h2>
<p>The UAE takes a significant volume of Indian cumin — roughly USD 90 million in 2024 — reached in just 3–6 days via Jebel Ali, among the fastest lanes we ship on for any product. What makes this market genuinely different from, say, China (India's largest cumin destination) is that a meaningful share of what lands at Jebel Ali doesn't stay in the UAE: the port functions as a major re-export hub into the wider Gulf, East Africa and Central Asia.</p>

<h2>Ask early if you're re-exporting</h2>
<p>If your cumin purchase is destined for onward sale beyond the UAE itself, tell us at the enquiry stage. It can change what packaging format and labelling makes sense — there's little point optimising for UAE domestic retail if the product is actually headed to a third market through a UAE-based trading relationship.</p>

<h2>Grade depends on the buyer type</h2>
<p>As with most spice markets, the UAE splits between bulk food-manufacturing buyers (machine-clean, 98%+ purity is generally sufficient) and buyers serving retail or re-export programmes who want sortex-clean, 99.5%+ purity. We match the grade to your stated end use rather than assuming.</p>

<h2>Currency and payment</h2>
<p>The AED's peg to the US dollar keeps currency risk low on this lane, and UAE trading houses are, as a group, experienced counterparties — documentation expectations are generally well understood on both sides, which tends to make the payment and terms conversation more straightforward here than in markets with less trade experience.</p>

